      ],
      "title": "Before a noun",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "A participle can also describe a noun, like an adjective. An adjective describes a noun. Before a noun, many participles add "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "-e",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " in plural or definite noun phrases."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Before a noun, participles often behave like adjectives: singular indefinite usually has the basic form, while plural and definite forms often end in "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "-e",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "."
            }
          ]
        },
